DOCTRINE CANDIDATES (3), from senechal's 2026-07-28 dead-config session (senechal 561af36, fc907d4). Filed rather than applied: BUILD-DISCIPLINE.md was NOT edited by that session, deliberately -- these want a human's call on whether they are patterns or just one project's anecdotes.

1. JUDGE A SUPERVISOR BY WHAT IT SUPERVISES, NOT BY ITS OWN LIVENESS. mandark's gardien.timer was 'active (waiting)', enabled, firing on schedule, and by every probe of ITSELF perfectly healthy -- while gardien.service, the thing it exists to trigger, exited 1 on every fire since 2026-07-24. Four nights unnoticed. The scheduler was never broken; its job does not include caring whether the work succeeded. Generalises past systemd to any supervisor/supervised pair: a wrapper that dispatches, a cron line, a retry loop, a queue consumer. The check must name the OUTCOME, not the mechanism. Related but distinct from Goodhart (that is a measure degrading once TARGETED; this one was never targeted, it was the wrong noun from the start). A citation hunt for this is filed with bibliothecaire (8562fb8) -- surrogate endpoints, liveness-vs-safety, IEC 61508 probability-of-failure-on-demand.

2. A COMPONENT WHOSE EXIT CODE IS ITS REPORT WILL BE MISREAD BY THE NEXT COMPONENT. senechal's health check exits 0/1/2/3 as its FINDING, so its unit declares SuccessExitStatus=1 2 3. This has now bitten TWICE from opposite directions in the same repo: once when a missing SuccessExitStatus meant every unhealthy run left a failed unit that the health check's own check_units then reported as a failure forever (fixed on the twenty-third nightly run), and again on 2026-07-28 when a NEW corpse-detector read ExecMainStatus and declared senechal's own timer dead within an hour of shipping. The rule that fixes both: read Result, not the raw exit code -- the authoritative verdict already accounts for what the unit declared as success. The wider pattern is the interesting part: an exit code that carries meaning to ITS caller is ambiguous to everyone else, and any repo doing this will keep re-hitting it at each new consumer until the convention is written down.

3. NOTICE-AND-NAG IS NOT A MECHANISM. senechal now detects dead machine config and reports it hourly with an exact retire command -- and nothing will ever run that command. An hourly WARN that is never actioned is the signal people learn to skim, and this same repo's finding 1 is about a component that failed loud for weeks with nobody listening. The underlying asymmetry, which is the part worth doctrine: INSTALLING something has a motivated actor and a deliberate moment; VERIFYING IT STILL WORKS and RETIRING IT have neither. Any protocol built only on push-notification-at-install-time inherits this hole by construction. Possible rule: a detector that cannot point at the thing which ACTS on its findings is half a mechanism, and should say so in its own docs. senechal's ESTATE.md now does say so explicitly, under 'How something actually gets sunset (the gap, stated honestly)'.
